{
  "document_coverage": "The source text explicitly states that weight quantization reduces memory by storing parameters with fewer bits, noting the trade-off where aggressive reduction degrades answer quality. It defines Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) as supplying relevant course passages to the language model's context. The mechanism involves a retrieval encoder mapping questions and documents into embeddings, followed by a similarity search selecting candidate passages from an index. It notes that updating the document index allows access to revised materials without retraining the model. Crucially, it states that retrieval does not change model parameters and warns that highly ranked passages can be irrelevant, outdated, or incorrect.",
